docs {
  # List products

  Returns the Lpaecomms product catalogue with the Gold Coast store location
  where each product is available. Backs two mashups: the interactive map and
  the currency converter. Mobile and desktop clients use the same endpoint.

  The catalogue itself comes from an external demo feed
  (`https://api.escuelajs.co/api/v1/products`); the store locations are merged
  in by the server, because the feed carries no coordinates.

  ## Pairing and truncation

  Products are paired with store locations **by index**: the feed's first
  product gets the first entry in the server's fixed `STORE_LOCATIONS` table,
  the second product gets the second entry, and so on. This is a positional
  pairing, not a real product-to-store relationship from a database — Lpaecomms
  has no product table yet — so it exists purely to give the map mashup one
  coordinate per marker.

  The response is truncated to the number of known store locations (currently
  five), even if the feed returns more. A product without a place on the map is
  not something the map mashup can display.

  ## AUD assumption

  `priceAud` reports the feed's `price` field unmodified, under the AUD
  currency code. The feed does not state a currency itself; treating its
  numbers as Australian dollars is an assumption made for this demonstration,
  not a property the upstream data actually carries.

  ## Caching

  Responses are cached in process for **5 minutes**, keyed by the catalogue
  URL. A single `/mashup` page load triggers two independent calls to this
  endpoint — one from the map mashup, one from the currency converter mashup —
  and the cache collapses them into one upstream call instead of two, so both
  mashups always see the same catalogue rather than two responses that could
  disagree if the feed changed in between. A failed upstream call is never
  cached and never evicts a still-valid cached entry, so one bad request cannot
  poison a good one for the rest of the TTL.

  ## Auth

  None. This endpoint is public.

  ## Parameters

  None.

  ## Responses

  ### 200 OK — catalogue returned

  An object holding an array of up to five products, in display order. Wrapped
  in an object rather than returned as a bare top-level array so metadata (for
  example pagination) can be added later without changing the response's root
  type — a bare array cannot grow that way without breaking every client at
  once.

  {
    "products": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "name": "Classic Red Pullover",
        "priceAud": 10,
        "imageUrl": "https://i.imgur.com/1twoaDy.jpeg",
        "store": "Surfers Paradise",
        "latitude": -28.0023,
        "longitude": 153.4145
      }
    ]
  }

  Fields:

  - `products` (array) — the catalogue, at most one entry per known store location.
  - `products[].id` (number) — product identifier from the catalogue feed.
  - `products[].name` (string) — product name.
  - `products[].priceAud` (number) — base price, assumed to be Australian dollars (see AUD assumption above).
  - `products[].imageUrl` (string) — product image; empty string when the feed has none.
  - `products[].store` (string) — Gold Coast store holding the product.
  - `products[].latitude` (number) — store latitude, WGS 84.
  - `products[].longitude` (number) — store longitude, WGS 84.

  ### 502 Bad Gateway — catalogue source unavailable

  The upstream catalogue is unreachable, timed out after 5 seconds, or answered
  a non-2xx status. The fault is upstream, not in this service.

  {
    "statusCode": 502,
    "error": "Bad Gateway",
    "message": "The product catalogue source is unavailable.",
    "validation": [],
    "url": "/api/v1/products",
    "method": "GET"
  }
}
